      { "text": "Global hypoxia triggers compensatory bronchoconstriction rather than the vasoconstriction seen in focal hypoxia", "explanation": "Incorrect. The vasoconstrictive response to low alveolar oxygen occurs in both cases; the difference is the extent of lung involved. This would be the correct answer if the effector mechanism switched with the size of the affected territory, which it does not." },
      { "text": "Focal hypoxia redistributes flow with little change in resistance; global hypoxia has nowhere to send blood", "explanation": "Correct. In focal hypoxia the resistance arteries feeding the poorly ventilated region squeeze shut and blood is shifted toward better-ventilated territory, with no significant increase in overall vascular resistance. In global hypoxia there is no better-ventilated territory to divert into, so the reflex simply constricts the entire bed at once. It becomes very hard for any part of the lung to take part in gas exchange, and the lecturer notes that when this is pathologic it may significantly alter vascular resistance, loading the heart until cardiac hypertrophy and often right-sided heart failure develop." }
